  #exercise[Under the notation of @ss:uniqueness-theorems $v in hat(V)_lambda$
    ($lambda != Lambda$) belongs to $M^((Lambda))$ if and only if
    $hat(rho)_Lambda (hat(e)_i) v in M^((Lambda))$ for all $i$. (This gives a
    recursive method for constructing
    $M^((Lambda))$.)] <exc:recursive-largest-submodule>

  #exercise[Under the notation of @ss:uniqueness-theorems let $Lambda$ be a
    highest weight of $rho$ and $lambda$ a dominant weight of the form
    $Lambda - alpha_(i_1) - dots.c - alpha_(i_k)$, where $alpha_(i_j) in Pi$.
    Then $lambda in Phi_rho$. (Hint: present $Lambda - lambda$ as the sum of a
    minimal number of positive roots:
    $Lambda - lambda = beta_1 + dots.c + beta_m$ and then by induction in $k$
    prove that $Lambda - beta_1 - dots.c - beta_k in Phi_rho$.)]
  <exc:dominant-weights-below-highest>

  Since any weight of $rho$ can be transformed into a dominant weight by a
  transformation from the Weyl group, we have a method for recovering all the
  weights of a representation from its highest weights.

  In
  Exercises~@exc:dual-representation-highest-weight)–@exc:orthogonal-symplectic-central-element)
  $R$ stands for a finite-dimensional linear representation of a connected
  semisimple Lie group $G$ in a space $V$, $rho = d R: frak(g) -> frak(g l)(V)$
  the corresponding tangent representation, $Pi$ a system of simple roots of
  $G$.

  #exercise[We have $Phi_(R^*) = -Phi_R$, where $R^*$ is the representation dual
    to $R$. If $R$ is the irreducible representation with the highest weight
    $Lambda$ and the lowest weight $M$ then $R^*$ is the irreducible
    representation with the highest weight
    $-M = -attach(w, tl: t, br: 0) Lambda$ and the lowest weight
    $-Lambda = -attach(w, tl: t, br: 0) M$, where $w_0 in W$ is the element
    defined in @ss:classifying-irreducible-representations.]
  <exc:dual-representation-highest-weight>

  #exercise[The transformation $nu = -attach(w, tl: t, br: 0)$ is an
    automorphism of $Pi$. If $frak(g)$ is simple and different from
    $frak(s l)_n (CC)$ ($n >= 3$), $frak(s o)_(4 n + 2) (CC)$ and $E_6$ then
    $nu = e$. For the remaining simple Lie algebras $nu$ is determined by the
    only nontrivial symmetry of the Dynkin diagram of $Pi$. If $frak(g)$ is not
    simple then $nu$ acts on $Pi$ componentwise. (Hint: apply
    Exercise~@exc:minus-identity-weyl-classification).]
  <exc:opposition-involution>

  A representation $R$ is called #idx(
    "representation",
    "self-dual",
  )_self-dual_ if $R^* tilde.op R$.

  #exercise[If $frak(g)$ is a simple Lie algebra different from
    $frak(s l)_n (CC)$, ($n >= 3$), $frak(s o)_(4 n + 2) (CC)$ or $E_6$ then any
    irreducible representation of $frak(g)$ is self-dual. For the remaining
    three types an irreducible representation is self-dual if and only if its
    numerical labels are symmetrically located on its Dynkin
    diagram.] <exc:self-dual-irreducible-criterion>

  #exercise[Let $H$ be the three-dimensional connected simple subgroup of $G$
    corresponding to the principal three-dimensional subalgebra
    $frak(h) subset frak(g)$ (see
    Exercise~@exc:principal-three-dimensional-subalgebra). If $R$ is an
    irreducible representation with the highest weight $Lambda$ and the lowest
    weight $M$ then there exists in $V$ a subspace invariant and irreducible
    with respect to $R(H)$ and containing $V_Lambda$, $V_M$. In this subspace,
    $R$ induces an irreducible representation $R_m$ of $H$, where
    $m = sum_(alpha in Pi) r_alpha Lambda_alpha$.]
  <exc:principal-sl2-restriction>

  #exercise[A linear representation $R$ is self-dual if and only if there exists
    a non-degenerate bilinear form on $V$ invariant with respect to
    $R(G)$.] <exc:self-dual-iff-invariant-form>

  #exercise[If for an irreducible representation $R$ there exists a nonzero
    bilinear form on $V$ invariant with respect to $R(G)$ then this form is
    non-degenerate and either symmetric or skew-symmetric; any two invariant
    bilinear forms are proportional to each
    other.] <exc:invariant-form-irreducible-unique>

  A linear representation $R$ is called #idx(
    "representation",
    "orthogonal",
  )_orthogonal_ if it preserves a non-degenerate symmetric invariant bilinear
  form and #idx("representation", "symplectic")_symplectic_ if it preserves a
  non-degenerate skew-symmetric invariant bilinear form. Any self-dual
  irreducible representation is, clearly, either orthogonal or symplectic.

  #exercise[#source(211)The representation $R_k$ of $SL_2 (CC)$ is orthogonal if
    $k$ is even and symplectic if $k$ is odd. Any irreducible representation of
    $SO_3 (CC)$ is orthogonal.] <exc:sl2-orthogonal-or-symplectic>

  #exercise[Let $R$ be an irreducible self-dual representation with highest
    weight $Lambda$ and $Lambda_alpha = Lambda(h_alpha)$ ($alpha in Pi$) the
    numerical labels of the highest weight. $R$ is orthogonal if
    $sum_(alpha in Pi) r_alpha Lambda_alpha$ is even and symplectic if this
    number is odd. Here $r_alpha$ are the coordinates of the vector
    $2 rho^or = sum_(alpha in Delta_frak(g)^+) alpha^or$ in the basis
    $Pi^or = {h_alpha: alpha in Pi}$. Thanks to
    Exercise~@exc:half-sum-positive-roots
    $rho^or = sum_(alpha in Pi) pi_(alpha^or)$, where
    $pi_(alpha^or) in frak(t)(RR)$ are the fundamental weights of the root
    system $Delta_frak(g)^or$.] <exc:orthogonal-symplectic-parity>

