#hints[
  #hint[@pr:generators-via-left-normed-commutators][By induction in the length
    $m$ of a word verify that the words of the form~@eq:left-normed-commutators
    constitute a subalgebra of $frak(a)$.]

  #hint[@pr:canonical-generators-relations][Make use of
    Problems~@pr:bracket-of-root-spaces and @pr:positive-root-chain-of-simple.]

  #hint[@pr:triangular-decomposition-g-hat][Making use of
    Problem~@pr:generators-via-left-normed-commutators and
    relation~@eq:chevalley-generator-relations show that
    $hat(frak(t)) + hat(frak(n))^+ + hat(frak(n))^-$ is a subalgebra of
    $hat(frak(g))$.]

  #hint[@pr:root-decomposition-g-hat][The identity
    $hat(frak(g))_0 = hat(frak(t))$ follows from the invertibility of $A$ and
    the linear independence of the elements $hat(h)_i$.]

  #hint[@pr:largest-ideal-avoiding-cartan][First prove that the ideal
    $frak(m) subset hat(frak(g))$ does not contain any $hat(h)_i$ if and only if
    $frak(m) inter hat(frak(t)) = 0$. Then show that any ideal $frak(m)$ is of
    the form $frak(m) = plus.o.big_alpha frak(m) inter hat(frak(g))_alpha$. To
    prove that $frak(m)^plus.minus$ are ideals notice that
    $
      [hat(f)_i, hat(frak(n))^+] subset hat(frak(t)) + hat(frak(n))^+, wide
      [hat(e)_i, hat(frak(n))^-] subset hat(frak(t)) + hat(frak(n))^-.
    $]

  #hint[@pr:kernel-is-largest-ideal][Follows from the fact that any non-zero
    ideal of $frak(g)$ contains at least one of the elements $h_i$.]

  #hint[@pr:highest-weight-exists][Apply Problem~@pr:root-vectors-shift-weights
    (more precisely, its generalization to any linear representations of
    $frak(g)$).]

  #hint[@pr:highest-weight-dominant][Let $r_i = r_(alpha_i)$ be the reflection
    corresponding to the root $alpha_i$. By Problem~@pr:alpha-string-of-weights
    $r_i (Lambda) = Lambda - Lambda_i alpha_i$ is a weight of the representation
    $rho$ in the subspace spanned by the vectors $v_(i_1 ... i_k)$,
    $v_emptyset$. By Problem~@pr:root-vectors-shift-weights
    $v_(i_1 ... i_k) in V_(Lambda - alpha_(i_1) - dots.c - alpha_(i_k))$. This
    implies that $Lambda_i >= 0$.]

  #hint[@pr:verma-module-g-hat-exists][Make use of
    Problem~@pr:free-lie-algebra-universal-property.]

  #hint[@pr:largest-ideal-in-kernel][Follows from the commutative diagram of
    Problem~@pr:intertwining-map-onto-irreducible.]

  #hint[@pr:h-hat-linearly-independent][Make use of the existence of the
    representation $hat(rho)_Lambda$ for any $Lambda_1, ..., Lambda_l$.]

  #hint[@pr:largest-ideal-acts-trivially][
    Problem~@pr:largest-ideal-avoiding-cartan implies that the subspace
    $rho'(frak(m)^-)(V)$ is $frak(g)$-invariant. If $rho'(frak(m)^-) != 0$ then
    $rho'(frak(m)^-)(V) = V$, $v_emptyset in rho'(frak(m)^-)(V)$ which
    contradicts Problem~@pr:quotient-module-highest-weight. To prove that
    $rho'(frak(m)^+) = 0$ consider the subspace
    $inter.big_(x in frak(m)^+) Ker rho'(x)$ invariant with respect to $frak(g)$
    and containing $v_emptyset$.]

  #hint[@pr:common-kernel-ad-e-trivial][Prove that the elements of the form
    $(ad f_(i_1)) ... (ad f_(i_p)) (ad h_(j_1)) ... (ad h_(j_q)) (x)$
    ($p, q >= 0$) form an ideal of $frak(g)$ belonging to $frak(n)^-$.]

  #hint[@pr:reordering-with-nilpotent-ad][#source(215)Expanding $(ad p)^l q = 0$
    we see that the product $p^l q$ is presented in the desired form.]

  #hint[@pr:lowering-highest-vector-vanishes][The vector
    $v = rho'(hat(f)_i)^(Lambda_i + 1) v_emptyset$ is obviously a weight vector
    of weight $Lambda - (Lambda_i + 1) alpha_i$. By a straightforward
    verification $rho(e_k) v = 0$ for all $k$.
    Problem~@pr:quotient-module-highest-weight shows that $v = 0$.]

  #hint[@pr:generators-locally-nilpotent][For $rho(e_i)$ this is clear from
    Problem~@pr:quotient-module-highest-weight. To prove the local nilpotency of
    $rho(f_i)$ consider the subspace $U_i = union.big_m Ker(rho(f_i))^m$.
    Problem~@pr:lowering-highest-vector-vanishes shows that $U_i != 0$. By a
    straightforward verification $U_i$ is invariant with respect to $rho(h_k)$
    and $rho(e_k)$ for all $k$. Finally, with the help of
    formula~@eq:serre-relations-in-representation and
    Problem~@pr:reordering-with-nilpotent-ad applied to $p = rho(f_i)$ and
    $q = rho(f_k)$, it is not difficult to show that $U_i$ is invariant with
    respect to $rho(f_k)$ for any $k$. This implies that $U_i = V$.]

  #hint[@pr:sl2-finite-invariant-subspaces][It suffices to prove that any weight
    vector belongs to a finite-dimensional subspace invariant with respect to
    $rho(frak(g)^((i)))$. Let $lambda in Omega$. Consider the subspace
    $
      U = V_lambda plus.o (plus.o.big_m rho(e_i)^m V_lambda) plus.o
      (plus.o.big_m rho(f_i)^m V_lambda).
    $
    It is easy to verify that $U$ is invariant with respect to
    $rho(frak(g)^((i)))$ and Problems~@pr:weight-spaces-finite-dimensional and
    @pr:generators-locally-nilpotent imply that $U$ is finite-dimensional.]

  #hint[@pr:sl2-element-induces-reflection][It suffices to consider two cases:
    $h = h_i$ and $alpha_i (h) = 0$. In the first case the statement of the
    problem reduces to the statement concerning $SL_2 (CC)$ which can be
    verified directly. In the second case it is necessary to prove that $rho(h)$
    commutes with $w_i$ but this follows from the fact that $rho(h)$ commutes
    with $rho(h_i), rho(e_i), rho(f_i)$.]

  #hint[@pr:weyl-orbit-meets-dominant][In the orbit of $gamma$ under $W$, choose
    an element $gamma_1$ with the minimal sum of the coefficients in its linear
    expression in terms of $alpha_1, ..., alpha_l$ and consider the elements
    $r_i (gamma_1)$.]

  #hint[@pr:weights-bounded-by-highest][Thanks to
    Problems~@pr:weights-reflection-invariant and @pr:weyl-orbit-meets-dominant
    we may assume that $(lambda, alpha_i) >= 0$ for all $i$. Making use of
    Problem~@pr:quotient-module-highest-weight we get
    $(Lambda - lambda, lambda) >= 0$.]

  #hint[@pr:regular-highest-weight-faithful][Under the conditions of the
    problem, $h_i in.not Ker rho$ for any $i$. Then apply
    Problem~@pr:nonzero-ideal-contains-coroot.]

  #hint[@pr:constructed-algebra-semisimple][Let $frak(a)$ be a commutative ideal
    of $frak(g)$ and $frak(a) != 0$. By
    Problem~@pr:nonzero-ideal-contains-coroot $h_i in frak(a)$ for some $i$ but
    then $e_i = 1/2 [h_i, e_i] in frak(a)$ contradicting the commutativity of
    $frak(a)$.]

  #hint[@pr:constructed-simple-roots][Make use of
    Problem~@pr:triangular-decomposition-g-hat and linear independence of the
    forms $alpha_1, ..., alpha_l$.]

  #hint[@pr:root-sl2-subgroups-simply-connected][The group $G^((k))$ is the
    image of the simply connected group $SL_2 (CC)$ with respect to the
    homomorphism $Phi_k = Phi_(alpha_k)$ (see
    @ss:three-dimensional-subalgebras[°]); it is simply connected if and only if
    $Phi_k$ is injective, i.e. $t_k = Phi_k (-E) != e$. It is easy to see that
    $t_k = exp(pi i h_k)$. The condition~@eq:characters-equal-weight-lattice
    implies the existence of a character $chi in cal(X)(T)$ such that
    $(d chi)(h_k) = 1$. Then $chi(t_k) = exp(pi i (d chi)(h_k)) = -1$ so that
    $t_k != e$.]

  #hint[@pr:torus-product-of-coroot-tori][Clearly, $T = T^((1)) ... T^((l))$.
    Let $t_k in T^((k))$ be elements such that $t_1 ... t_l = e$.
    Condition~@eq:characters-equal-weight-lattice implies the existence of
    characters $chi_i in cal(X)(T)$ ($i = 1, ..., l$), such that
    $d chi_i = pi_i$ (fundamental weights). We have
    $chi_k (t_1 ... t_l) = chi_k (t_k) = 1$. Let $t_k = exp(2 pi i c_k h_k)$,
    where $c_k in CC$. Then $chi_k (t_k) = exp(2 pi i c_k)$ so that $c_k in ZZ$
    and $t_k = e$.]

  #hint[@pr:torus-fundamental-group-surjects][Make use of
    Theorems~@th:homotopy-sequence-homogeneous-space,
    @th:flag-variety-simply-connected and
    Problem~@pr:borel-semidirect-decomposition.]

  #hint[@pr:linearity-reduce-simply-connected][Make use of
    Theorem~@th:chevalley-line-stabilizer.]

  #hint[@pr:weights-generating-weight-lattice][#source(216)Such is e.g. the sum
    of the irreducible representations with highest weights $pi_1, ..., pi_l$
    existing thanks to Theorem~@th:highest-weight-representation-exists. The
    representation is faithful thanks to
    Problem~@pr:weights-integral-on-coroots.]

  #hint[@pr:prove-center-via-characters][Make use of the fact that
    $Z(G) = {exp x: x in frak(t), e^(alpha(x)) = 1 " for all "
      alpha in Delta_frak(g)}$.]

  #hint[@pr:covering-maps-maximal-tori][Make use of the relation
    $Ker p subset Z(tilde(G))$.]

  #hint[@pr:prove-fundamental-group-formula][Make use of
    Diagram~@eq:covering-tori-diagram.]

  #hint[@pr:lattice-preserving-isomorphism-lifts][Let $p_i: tilde(G)_i -> G_i$
    be a simply connected covering of $G_i$ ($i = 1, 2$) and $T_i subset G_i$,
    $tilde(T)_i subset tilde(G)_i$ the maximal tori corresponding to
    $frak(t)_i$. The isomorphism $phi$ determines an isomorphism
    $tilde(Phi): tilde(G)_1 -> tilde(G)_2$ such that the diagram
    $
      #cd(
        (
          A: (0, 0, $tilde(T)_1$),
          B: (0, 3, $tilde(T)_2$),
          C: (1, 1, $frak(t)_1$),
          D: (1, 2, $frak(t)_2$),
          E: (2, 0, $T_1$),
          F: (2, 3, $T_2$),
        ),
        (
          ar("A", "B", label: $tilde(Phi)$),
          ar("A", "E", label: $p_1$, side: "right"),
          ar("B", "F", label: $p_2$),
          ar("C", "A", label: $tilde(cal(E))_1$, side: "right"),
          ar("D", "B", label: $tilde(cal(E))_2$),
          ar("C", "E", label: $cal(E)_1$),
          ar("D", "F", label: $cal(E)_2$, side: "right"),
          ar("C", "D", label: $phi$),
        ),
        column: 20mm,
        row: 16mm,
      )
    $
    where $cal(E)_i$, $tilde(cal(E))_i$ are defined as in
    @eq:torus-exponential-map, commutes (see
    Problem~@pr:covering-maps-maximal-tori).
    Theorem~@th:fundamental-group-via-characters implies that
    $tilde(Phi)(Ker p_1) = Ker p_2$ so that $tilde(Phi)$ determines the desired
    isomorphism $Phi: G_1 -> G_2$.]

  #hint[@pr:prove-global-uniqueness][Make use of
    Theorem~@th:cartan-matrix-determines-algebra and
    Problem~@pr:lattice-preserving-isomorphism-lifts.]

  #hint[@pr:semisimple-groups-cartan-subgroup-pairs][Let $A$ be the Cartan
    matrix of $frak(g)$. It follows from
    @eq:simple-roots-via-fundamental-weights that the isomorphism $ZZ^l -> P$
    sending the set $(k_1, ..., k_l)$ to $sum_(1 <= i <= l) k_i pi_i$ maps $Q_A$
    onto $Q$ and therefore induces an isomorphism $pi(A) -> pi(Delta_frak(g))$.
    With the subgroup $cal(X)(T)\/Q subset pi(Delta_frak(g))$ associated is an
    isomorphic subgroup $Z subset pi(A)$.
    Theorems~@th:global-uniqueness-semisimple-groups and
    @th:global-existence-semisimple-groups imply that the correspondence
    $G |-> (A, Z)$ determines the desired bijection.]

  #hint[@pr:prove-group-highest-weight][The uniqueness of the representation
    with given highest weight follows from
    Theorems~@th:highest-weight-determines-irreducible and
    @th:homomorphism-determined-by-differential. Let $Lambda in cal(X)(T)$ be a
    dominant character. By Theorem~@th:highest-weight-representation-exists
    there exists an irreducible finite-dimensional linear representation
    $rho: frak(g) -> frak(g l)(V)$ of $frak(g)$ with the highest weight
    $Lambda$. By Theorem~@th:homomorphism-existence-simply-connected
    $rho = d tilde(R)$ for an irreducible representation
    $tilde(R): tilde(G) -> GL(V)$, where $tilde(G)$ is a simply connected
    covering group of $G$. By Theorem~@th:fundamental-group-via-characters the
    kernel of the covering $phi: tilde(G) -> G$ is of the form
    $Ker phi = tilde(cal(E))(frak(t)(ZZ))$. On the other hand, it follows from
    Problem~@pr:highest-weight-exists that $Phi_(tilde(R)) subset cal(X)(T)$
    whence we derive that $tilde(R)(Ker phi) = e$ so that $tilde(R)$ determines
    the desired linear representation $R: G -> GL(V)$.]

  #hint[@pr:longest-element-highest-to-lowest][Let $B^+$ and $B^-$ be the Borel
    subgroups of $G$ corresponding to the Weyl chambers $C_0$ and $-C_0$ (see
    @ss:borel-subgroups-and-maximal-tori[°]). It follows from
    Problem~@pr:automorphisms-act-on-weights-roots that $n_0 B^+ n_0^(-1) = B^-$
    and $n_0 B^- n_0^(-1) = B^+$.]
]
